<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=rammed-earth">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rammed earth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 5000 BCE</span> · Also known as pisé, hāng tǔ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rammed earth, also called pisé, is a technique for building foundations, floors and walls by compacting a damp mixture of natural raw materials such as earth, chalk, lime or gravel into a formwork. Evidence of its ancient use has been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a>, dating to the 9th to 7th millennium BCE, and at Yangshao and Longshan sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, dating to 5000 BCE. By 2000 BCE rammed-earth techniques (hāng tǔ) were commonly used for walls and foundations in China. Historically, additives such as lime or animal blood were used to stabilise the earth, and wooden planks tied with rope served as formwork.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rammed-earth.jpg" alt="Rammed earth" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Real Bear · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Summer_Vacation_2007,_263,_Watchtower_In_The_Morning_Light,_Dunhuang,_Gansu_Province.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its builders laid out palaces around courtyards, the largest covering 9,600 m², and enclosed them with a wall of <b>rammed earth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>It has three levels, the highest a platform of <b>rammed earth</b>, with three further platforms paved with cobblestones and the remains of a stone wall.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Builders raised town walls of <b>rammed earth</b> and improved the making of wells: the Hougang II variant of northern Henan and southern Hebei has the first wells of the Yellow River area, dug by a method that early Bronze Age states there went on using.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late period Xishan in central Henan, a settlement of 25 hectares near modern Zhengzhou, received the first wall of <b>rammed earth</b> in China, and the first walled town of the Yellow River valley appeared.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yangshao-culture">Yangshao culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Walls were raised in <b>rammed earth</b>, lime concrete or brick and plastered, roofs and ceilings made of pine, columns and fountains of white Macael marble.</blockquote><a href="/work/alhambra">Alhambra · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The walls have a <b>rammed earth</b> core faced on both sides with three layers of baked brick.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 5000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Yangshao and Longshan sites, China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Fertile Crescent (9th to 7th millennium BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Evidence at Neolithic sites in the Fertile Crescent dates to the 9th to 7th millennium BCE, which would be earlier than the Chinese sites, but no dated site is given, so the card uses the explicit date of 5000 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mudbrick<span>c. 9000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=rammed-earth">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
